{
  "answer_text": "Registry-listed public MCP servers provide quick discovery, easier integration, and reduce hosting/maintenance burden—making them useful for experimentation and rapid onboarding—but you give up control over availability, latency, privacy, security posture, versioning cadence, and SLAs. Hosting a custom/self-hosted MCP server gives full control over authentication, access control, data handling, tooling, and guaranteed SLAs, but shifts responsibilities to your team: designing and operating the server (scaling, monitoring, backups), implementing capability discovery and schema/version compatibility, enforcing authz and tenant isolation, sandboxing and vetting tools/resources, and maintaining compliance and upgrades. For production, prefer a custom or strictly vetted/allowlisted registry entry with clear SLAs and governance; use public registry entries only for non-sensitive or low-risk use cases where speed-to-market outweighs control.",
  "source_quote": "Central catalog of publicly available MCP servers for discovery and implementation"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Treat discovered registry entries as untrusted until vetted; enforce endpoint validation (TLS certificates/hostnames and signed manifests), capability and schema verification, strong authentication/authorization (short‑lived tokens or mTLS and least‑privilege scopes), sandboxing and execution limits, allowlist/denylist and operator approval flows, comprehensive logging/auditing with revoke/quarantine capability, and explicit user/operator consent before sending PII. These controls are necessary because registry discovery provides availability but not trust—servers may misbehave, differ in capabilities, or expose sensitive data if not properly constrained.",
  "source_quote": "Central catalog of publicly available MCP servers for discovery and implementation"
}

